## Compile Gate and Headless Test Runs

Discovered while implementing the Room/Pit piece types (`.superpowers/sdd/roompit-task-*-report.md`).

The fast, reliable way to check the C# compiles is:

```bash
dotnet build Palisade.csproj -v q
```

This runs in a few seconds. The `.csproj` **must be named explicitly** —
the repo root holds both `Palisade.csproj` and `palisade-godot.sln`, and an
unqualified `dotnet build` resolves ambiguously.

**Use the `_console.exe` wrapper for anything whose output you need.** The
main `Godot_v4.6.2-stable_mono_win64.exe` is a GUI-subsystem binary: invoked
from PowerShell it DETACHES immediately (the call returns with only the
version banner, `$LASTEXITCODE` stays empty, and the process keeps running in
the background — dangerous right before an export, which must never overlap a
running instance). The sibling
`C:\Godot_v4.6.2-stable_mono_win64\Godot_v4.6.2-stable_mono_win64_console.exe`
is a console wrapper that blocks until exit and forwards all test/engine
output plus the real exit code. Use it for headless test scenes, e.g.:

```powershell
& "C:\Godot_v4.6.2-stable_mono_win64\Godot_v4.6.2-stable_mono_win64_console.exe" --path . --headless res://test/TestGateSync.tscn
```

(Passing the scene path directly also avoids touching `run/main_scene`.)

Two Godot invocations that look like reasonable alternatives are traps:

- `godot --headless --path . --quit` can hang past 300 seconds and must not
  be used as a compile gate. Use the `dotnet build` command above instead,
  then only launch Godot (headless or not) once the build is already known
  to be green.
- `godot --headless --path . --write-movie ...` crashes this engine build
  with a null texture dereference under the GL-compatibility renderer. For
  scripted screenshot/video capture, run Godot **without** `--headless`,
  writing a PNG sequence instead:

  ```bash
  "$GODOT_MONO" --path . --write-movie "screenshots/mydir/frame.png" --fixed-fps 1 --quit-after N
  ```

  Point `run/main_scene` in `project.godot` at the target test scene first
  (back it up, `sed` the one line, restore it immediately after capture —
  never leave `project.godot` pointed at a test scene or staged with that
  change).

A headless run against a scene whose C# hasn't been rebuilt will silently
reuse the last compiled assembly rather than erroring — always run the
`dotnet build` compile gate first, not a Godot run, when checking whether an
edit actually compiles.

## Exporting the Playtest Build (Windows)

Discovered the hard way across the 2026-09-22 playtest rounds (see JOURNAL.md
entries from that date).

**The sequence — order matters:**

1. **Close the Godot editor.** Running headless `--import`/`--export-release`
   while the editor holds the project corrupts `.godot/imported/` (two
   instances fight over the cache) and ships stale/missing assets. Check:
   `Get-Process | Where-Object { $_.ProcessName -like "*odot*" }`
2. Verify `run/main_scene` in project.godot is `res://scenes/TitleScreen.tscn`
   — the export bakes whatever is there.
3. Import, then export, with the mono binary:
   ```powershell
   & "C:\Godot_v4.6.2-stable_mono_win64\Godot_v4.6.2-stable_mono_win64.exe" --path . --headless --import
   & "C:\Godot_v4.6.2-stable_mono_win64\Godot_v4.6.2-stable_mono_win64.exe" --path . --headless --export-release "Corners" "build/windows/Corners.exe"
   ```
4. Distribute the WHOLE folder — `Corners.exe` needs the sibling
   `data_Palisade_windows_x86_64/`:
   ```powershell
   Compress-Archive -Path 'build\windows\Corners.exe','build\windows\data_Palisade_windows_x86_64' -DestinationPath 'build\Corners-Windows.zip' -Force
   ```

**Export-only failure class to remember:** exports pack IMPORTED artifacts,
not source files. Any code that reads assets via `Image.Load`,
`GltfDocument.AppendFromFile`, `System.IO`, or `GlobalizePath` works in the
editor and silently fails in the exe — always go through
`ResourceLoader`/`GD.Load` first (see PropLibrary.PropsAvailable and
DungeonBuilder.LoadTexture for the pattern). A `.gdignore` over a directory
keeps it out of exports entirely; `assets/glb/props_hires/` is deliberately
ignored (661 MB raw AI meshes — the game reads `assets/glb/props/`).

## Local Multiplayer Testing

- `node --test server/test/relay.test.js` — relay end-to-end tests (1v1 characterization + FFA parties) over real WebSockets on port 3199; no Godot needed. On Windows pass the file, not the directory.
- `test-local.bat` — starts the local relay (node) + two windows (Alice/Bob)
  against `ws://localhost:3000`, tiled side by side.
- `test-online.bat` — two windows through the PRODUCTION Render relay: real
  machine → Render → machine round trips for genuine latency feel.
- Both use `--test-windowed` (960×540 windowed regardless of the saved
  fullscreen preference, without overwriting it) plus `--position` to tile.
- **Automated two-client run** (no export needed): `test/TestOnlineFlow`
  drives the real matchmaking → PreGameLobby → OnlineDungeonArena → rematch
  path on two instances against a local relay on a spare port. Throwaway
  accounts only (it writes maze slot 3). The client deliberately loads the
  lobby 1.5 s late. Both print PASS/FAIL and `ALL PASS`:
  ```bash
  PORT=3137 node server/relay.js &
  G=/c/Godot_v4.6.2-stable_mono_win64/Godot_v4.6.2-stable_mono_win64_console.exe
  "$G" --path . --headless res://test/TestOnlineFlow.tscn --account __test_online_host   --relay ws://localhost:3137 --role host &
  "$G" --path . --headless res://test/TestOnlineFlow.tscn --account __test_online_client --relay ws://localhost:3137 --role client &
  wait
  ```
  Drop `--headless` on the host and add `--shot <dir>` to save
  `sign_start.png` / `sign_exit.png` from the rematch. Stop the relay after.
- **FFA lobby, 4 clients** (`test/TestFfaLobbyFlow`). Throwaway `__test_ffa_*` accounts only;
  each writes its own slot 3. It covers:
  - host creates a party and invites the other three from the lobby's invite list;
  - seat cards and pick sync;
  - START gating;
  - `ffa_start` → `OnlineFfaArena` with the right seat and roster;
  - one client leaves → `peer_left`.
  ```bash
  PORT=3138 node server/relay.js & RP=$!
  P=""; for r in host c1 c2 c3; do
    "$G" --path . --headless res://test/TestFfaLobbyFlow.tscn --account __test_ffa_$r --relay ws://localhost:3138 --role $r > ffa-$r.log 2>&1 & P="$P $!"; done
  for p in $P; do wait $p; done; kill $RP
  grep -h "ALL PASS\|FAIL" ffa-*.log
  ```
  (Wait only on the Godot PIDs; a bare `wait` would also wait on the relay.) Never while League is running.
- Exported-game logs land in
  `%APPDATA%\Godot\app_userdata\Corners\logs\godot.log` — the first place to
  look when a playtest misbehaves ("[Gun] HIT", "[ARENA] ...", rejected hit
  claims, prop availability all print there).

## FFA Plus Layout (local, no networking)

Headless suites (each prints PASS/FAIL lines and `ALL PASS`, exit code 0/1):

```bash
G=/c/Godot_v4.6.2-stable_mono_win64/Godot_v4.6.2-stable_mono_win64_console.exe
for t in TestMazeRotation TestMazeFootprint TestPlusLayout TestArenaDoors TestHallway; do
  "$G" --path . --headless res://test/$t.tscn; done
```

- `TestArenaDoors` compares against `test/golden/arena_doors.txt`, a fingerprint of the
  N/S arena recorded on the PRE-door-refactor ArenaBuilder (af13e62). A mismatch is a real
  1v1 regression. Never re-record on current code: temporarily check out af13e62's
  ArenaBuilder.cs, record with `-- record`, then restore it.
- `TestMazeFootprint` measures how far built geometry overhangs its cells and fails if it
  exceeds `MazeFootprint.Margin` (1.0 m; measured worst 0.95 m, a wall candelabra).

Dev scene: `test/FfaLocalArena.tscn -- fixture=worst|central|mixed|t3|vsep|tall|heaviest`
(`slots=1,2,3,4`, `spawn=arena|0..3`, `noplayer`). It prints `[FFA] ... hall=N cells`.

Captures (windowed; **never while League is running**; on the non-League monitor):
```bash
"$G" --path . --screen 1 --resolution 1080x1080 res://test/TestFfaCapture.tscn -- mode=overhead fixture=worst
"$G" --path . --screen 1 --resolution 1600x900  res://test/TestFfaCapture.tscn -- mode=perf fixture=heaviest
"$G" --path . --screen 1 --resolution 1600x900  res://test/TestFfaCapture.tscn -- mode=perf1v1
```
PNGs land in `screenshots/ffa/` (gitignored). Perf mode forces vsync off and `Engine.MaxFps=0`
and prints the readback. The `tall` fixture takes ~10 min per perf run.

## Claude Chat Export to Obsidian

This repo is also an Obsidian vault. Claude Code transcript archives can be exported locally with no API calls or LLM token cost:

```powershell
.\tools\export-claude-chats.ps1
```

If PowerShell script execution is disabled on the machine, use the per-command bypass form:

```powershell
powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\tools\export-claude-chats.ps1
```

By default the script reads transcript JSONL files from:

```txt
C:\Users\Abe\.claude\projects
```

and writes Markdown files to:

```txt
.\AI Chats\Claude Code
```

Useful options:

```powershell
.\tools\export-claude-chats.ps1 -DryRun
.\tools\export-claude-chats.ps1 -ProjectFilter palisade
.\tools\export-claude-chats.ps1 -Force
.\tools\export-claude-chats.ps1 -OutputRoot "D:\Obsidian\AI Chats\Claude Code"
```

The exporter keeps `.export-manifest.json` in the output folder and skips unchanged transcripts unless `-Force` is passed.

By default the exporter is Obsidian-friendly: it skips Claude thinking blocks, tool-use JSON, and tool results, and truncates very large individual messages. This prevents the vault from freezing on huge raw transcript dumps. For a full raw archive, export outside an Obsidian vault and pass:

```powershell
powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\tools\export-claude-chats.ps1 -IncludeThinking -IncludeToolResults -MaxEntryChars 0 -OutputRoot "C:\Users\Abe\Documents\Claude Raw Archive"
```

For Claude Code hook automation, use `tools/export-claude-hook.ps1`. It reads Claude hook JSON from stdin, extracts `transcript_path`, and exports only that session:

```powershell
powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\tools\export-claude-hook.ps1
```

This machine has a global Claude `SessionEnd` hook installed in:

```txt
C:\Users\Abe\.claude\settings.json
```

The hook runs the global exporter script from:

```txt
C:\Users\Abe\.claude\tools\export-claude-hook.ps1
```

It writes all project exports into the main personal Obsidian vault:

```txt
C:\Users\Abe\Documents\NoBrainer\AI Chats\Claude Code
```

Palisade can still keep project-local exports in `.\AI Chats\Claude Code` when running the exporter manually with `-OutputRoot`.
